course: dictionary
lesson: 8153
title: Reading it again
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To reread means to read something again. You might reread a sentence that did not make sense the first time, or reread a question before you answer it. «You do not need to reread the whole text for every question.» Often one sentence or one paragraph is enough.
explain: Rereading is a strategy, which means a plan you use when you get stuck. Before you answer, reread the question so you know exactly what it asks. After you write, reread your own work to spot mistakes and fix them.
explain: Rereading helps in maths too. When a word problem has lots of words, reread it slowly and find the numbers that matter. Ask yourself: what is the question asking me to find?
fact: Reread means to read again. The re- part means again, and read means to look at words and understand them.
fact: Reread can be a verb (I reread the page) or a noun (a reread is a second reading of a book).
fact: Rereading a difficult sentence slowly often makes its meaning clear.
fact: It helps to reread the question before you answer, so you do not miss what it asks.
fact: You do not have to reread a whole text for every question. Reread only the part you need.
exercise: What does reread mean? | To skip a page | To throw a book away | To read something aloud for the first time | * To read something again
exercise: Why might you reread a maths word problem? | To decide not to answer it | * To find the numbers that matter | To make the problem longer | To read the answer first
exercise: Find the word in this sentence that means read again: «Before you hand in your story, reread it to find mistakes.» | * reread | mistakes | story | hand
test: «You do not need to reread the whole text for every question.» Why not? | * Because you only need to reread the part that answers the question | Because questions never need answers | Because texts are always short | Because rereading is against the rules
test: In this sentence, what part of speech is reread? «A reread of the book helped me see the clues.» | * noun | adjective | adverb | verb
exam: When you reread your own writing, what are you looking for? | * Mistakes you can fix | Words to make it longer for no reason | A new title that changes the whole story | Nothing, you never need to check
